userStories.description:
**Goal** — Disable hooks for nax-owned bot git operations while retaining shared git hardening and timeouts.

**Motivation** — With a relative `core.hooksPath` (husky v9 writes `.husky/_` into `.git/config`), git resolves hooks inside the new worktree, which holds the PR head's content.

**Approach** — No hooks on bot git (S1). A new leaf module `src/bot/git.ts` exports `botGit`: same signature as `gitWithTimeout`, but always runs `git -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null <args>` through `gitWithTimeout`'s `argvOverride` parameter, so the fsmonitor and submodule hardening and the timeout handling still apply. Every bot dependency object with a `runGit` member (`_checkoutDeps`, `_fixInputDeps`, `_fixStepsDeps`, `_gateBaselineDeps`, `_savedFixDeps`, `_botReviewDeps`, `_botChecksDeps`, `_baseConfigDeps`) defaults it to `botGit`. Both pushes (`pushStep`, and `checkPushAccess`'s dry run) also pass `--no-verify`. The change is bot-only: `nax run` keeps running hooks where its config asks for them (`tdd.testWriterCommitHooks: "run"`). The `["git", "-c", ...]` argv literal in `git.ts` carries `// nax-git-env-allow: handed to gitWithTimeout, which hardens it` for `check-git-spawn-env.ts`.

**Interface** — `src/bot/git.ts`:
```ts
import { gitWithTimeout } from "@/utils/git";

/** Bot git never runs hooks: a PR head can supply them through a relative core.hooksPath. */
export const BOT_GIT_HOOKS_OFF = ["-c", "core.hooksPath=/dev/null"] as const;

export function botGit(
  args: string[],
  workdir: string,
  timeoutMs?: number,
  maxBytes?: number,
): ReturnType<typeof gitWithTimeout> {
  // nax-git-env-allow: handed to gitWithTimeout, which hardens it
  return gitWithTimeout(args, workdir, timeoutMs, maxBytes, ["git", ...BOT_GIT_HOOKS_OFF, ...args]);
}
```

Read or called, unchanged: `gitWithTimeout(args, workdir, timeoutMs?, maxBytes?, argvOverride?)` from `@/utils/git` (re-exported from `@nathapp/nax-agent/internal`). It spawns `hardenedGitArgv(argvOverride ?? ["git", ...args])` with `hardenedGitEnv(process.env)`; `hardenedGitArgv` skips `-c <value>` pairs when it looks for the verb. `BOT_GIT_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_MS` (120 000) from `src/bot/checkout.ts`.

`pushStep` (`src/bot/fix-steps.ts`). Target argv: `push --no-verify origin HEAD:refs/heads/<headRef>`. `checkPushAccess` (`src/bot/fix-input.ts`). Target argv: `push --dry-run --no-verify origin HEAD:refs/heads/<headRef>`.

Export `botGit` and `BOT_GIT_HOOKS_OFF` from `src/bot/index.ts`. Update runner imports/defaults in checkout, fix-input, fix-steps, gate-baseline, saved-fix, review-deps, checks and base-config. Preserve cwd/refspec and 120 000ms network budgets. Update existing base-config runner identity and push argv assertions, retaining their original timeout invariants. Build real-git hook fixtures with a separate bare origin and committed executable relative .husky/_ hooks, using an external marker and an unwrapped positive control.

**Scope** — In: the botGit leaf, all eight bot runGit defaults, both push flags, barrel exports and associated unit/real-git integration tests. Out: US-002's leftover auto-commit runner injection; US-003's run-unique fetch refs and cleanup; US-004–US-006's writer authorization and artifacts. Hooks are not disabled for `nax run`, the workflow, or any git outside a bot command; `hardenedGitEnv` is unchanged. A trusted review's setup command (for example `npm ci` running husky) can still write `core.hooksPath` into the shared repo config; trusted reviews may run head code by ruling S8. `readHead` in the bot fix runs `rev-parse` through the workflow's git dep, not `botGit`; `rev-parse` runs no hooks.

**Goal** — Add reusable cached writer authorization and make ruling permission reads delegate to it without changing ruling semantics.

**Motivation** — Write access is checked only for rulings (`permissionReader` in `src/bot/rulings.ts`).

**Approach** — A new module `src/bot/writers.ts` exports `writerCheck`, a per-login cached wrapper of `hasWriteAccess` where a failed check counts as "not a writer" (fail closed) and is reported through an injected `warn` sink, and `readWriters`, which returns the logins with write access among the authors of the comments it is given, forwarding each comment's `authorId`. `permissionReader` in `rulings.ts` delegates to `writerCheck` with its existing warning, so write access is decided in one place.

**Interface** — `src/bot/writers.ts`:
```ts
export type WriterCheck = (who: { readonly login: string; readonly id?: number }) => Promise<boolean>;

export function writerCheck(
  input: { readonly ref: PrRef; readonly deps: ForgeDeps; readonly cwd: string },
  check: typeof hasWriteAccess,
  warn: (login: string, error: string) => void,
): WriterCheck; // caches the promise per login; a rejected check calls warn(login, message) once and resolves false

export async function readWriters(
  comments: readonly PrComment[],
  check: WriterCheck,
): Promise<ReadonlySet<string>>; // distinct logins whose check resolves true; passes { login: author, id: authorId }
```

Read or called, unchanged: `hasWriteAccess(ref, { login, id? }, deps, cwd): Promise<boolean>` from `@/forge`; on GitLab it throws `FORGE_RESPONSE_UNPARSEABLE` when `id` is missing. `PrComment` (`author`, `authorId?`), `PrThread` (`comments`) from `@/forge`.

`permissionReader` (`src/bot/rulings.ts`, file-local) returns `(comment) => check({ login: comment.author, id: comment.authorId })` where `check = writerCheck(...)` is built on a closure that reads `_rulingsDeps.hasWriteAccess` at call time (tests swap it), whose `warn` calls the existing `warn("Could not check ruling permission", { login, error })` helper (stage `bot-rulings`, `_rulingsDeps.getLogger`).

Export `writerCheck`, `readWriters` and `type WriterCheck` from `src/bot/index.ts`. Cache the in-flight promise per login, not only a settled boolean, so concurrent callers share one request and one warning. Convert rejected permission lookups using the existing errorMessage helper and resolve false. `hasWriteAccess` throws for a login: that login is not a writer; the `warn` sink is called once for the login. GitLab comment has no `authorId`: `hasWriteAccess` throws; the author is not a writer. Add module unit tests and preserve the existing recordPrRulings failure-warning stage/data through its injectable logger.

**Scope** — In: writerCheck/readWriters/WriterCheck, bot barrel exports and permissionReader delegation. Out: US-005 owns agreed-item policy, rendering and artifact version 3; US-006 owns triage caller selection and legacy artifact migration; US-001–US-003 own git execution and base binding. S4, a non-writer steering a `fixed` ruling on a `w-` item through prompt text, is a separate spec.
